 ## Overview
-This method accelerates radiance field training by dynamically adjusting ray distribution through context-based probability sampling and adaptive quadtree subdivision [S1]. It reduces training time by 15–40% while maintaining or improving reconstruction accuracy compared to baseline methods like NeRF, Plenoxels, and Instant-NGP [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that use fixed block sizes or uniform sampling within blocks, this approach subdivides image regions into variable-sized leaf nodes based on rendering error, allowing for finer resolution in complex areas and coarser sampling in converged regions [S1].
+This method accelerates radiance field training by dynamically adjusting ray distribution through a quadtree structure on input views [S1]. It reduces the number of unnecessary network evaluations in low-detail regions while maintaining accuracy in complex areas, achieving 15–40% faster training times compared to baselines like NeRF, Plenoxels, and Instant-NGP [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that use fixed block sizes or uniform sampling within blocks, this approach subdivides leaf nodes based on rendering error and samples rays according to image context probability [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates on a per-view basis, constructing a spatial hierarchy to guide ray sampling. It combines a prior probability distribution derived from image context with an error-driven subdivision mechanism.
+The method operates within a standard volume rendering training loop (e.g., NeRF [S1]). It consists of two main components: a probability-based sampling function for initial ray selection and an adaptive quadtree subdivision strategy for dynamic refinement [S1].
 
